;; CHECK-FAIL: "partial"
;;
;; Endeavor 150 rung 7 — fusing on `acc` inside a STAGED K-loop must be REFUSED.
;; NEGATIVE by design, and it should stay negative forever.
;;
;; THE HAZARD.  In the staged pattern, matrix-multiply-tile-stride's grid-k loop calls
;; mma-accumulate-via-tile ONCE PER K-STEP, and the register C-tile carries the running sum
;; ACROSS those calls.  So inside the reduction body `acc` is a PARTIAL sum, and a map applied
;; there mutates the accumulator that subsequent K-steps keep adding to.
;;
;; NOTE WHAT THIS RUNG DELIBERATELY USES: a LINEAR function.  It is tempting to state the rule
;; as "non-linear activations are wrong on a partial sum" — that is the intuitive version, and
;; it is TOO WEAK.  Even doubling is wrong, because the corruption comes from the accumulator
;; being re-transformed on every step, not from the shape of the function.  With p1 and p2 the
;; two K-steps' contributions:
;;
;;     correct   (map once, post-reduction, in the :epilogue):
;;         C = p1 + p2, then x2                     ->  2*p1 + 2*p2
;;
;;     this kernel (map per K-step, on the running accumulator):
;;         k=0:  C = p1,          then x2  ->  2*p1
;;         k=1:  C = 2*p1 + p2,   then x2  ->  4*p1 + 2*p2
;;
;; The ONLY function that survives per-K-step application is the identity.  So the refusal is
;; not a linearity test — it is structural, and it covers every function including this one.
;;
;; WHY REFUSE RATHER THAN WARN.  The failure is silent: a plausible-looking matmul that is
;; simply wrong, in a build where the only thing the user changed was adding an epilogue.  This
;; project has been bitten by that class repeatedly (135/01's atomics returning 0.0; the
;; fragment adjoint that fell through a `when`).  The user also has a correct alternative
;; sitting right there — move the map into the :epilogue, where the C-tile is complete — so a
;; refusal costs them nothing but a one-line move.
;;
;; The compiler can DECIDE this structurally: %mmts-split-epilogue (src/analysis/control.lisp)
;; already splits the body into reduction-body and epilogue-body, so "is this via-tile call in
;; the reduction half" is known at analysis time.
;;
;; (If a deliberate per-K-step transform is ever genuinely wanted — some stability clamp, say —
;; it should be an explicit opt-in, not the default reading of this code.)
;;
;; NOTE ON THE EXPECTED MESSAGE.  The substring is the CONTRACT, chosen before the
;; implementation exists (149 rung 03's convention).  The diagnostic must say that the
;; accumulator is a PARTIAL sum here and point at the :epilogue as the correct site, or the
;; user cannot act on it.

(def-type a-mat (matrix float :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))
(def-type b-mat (matrix float :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :col-major))
(def-type c-mat (matrix float :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))

;; LINEAR on purpose — see the header.  Even this must be refused.
(def-function double-val (x)
  (declare #'(float => float))
  (* x 2.0))

(def-kernel map_on_partial_sum (A B &out C)
  (declare #'(a-mat b-mat &out c-mat)
           (global-size :set-to 32)
           (local-size  :set-to 32))
  (let ((A-tile (make-scratch-matrix float (16 8)))
        (B-tile (make-scratch-matrix float (8 8)))
        (C-tile (make-register-tile float (16 8) 0.0))
        (K      (inner-dimension A B)))
    (matrix-multiply-tile-stride C C-tile K 8 (grid-y grid-x grid-k)
      (load-tile A A-tile (grid-y grid-k))
      (load-tile B B-tile (grid-k grid-x))
      (sync-workgroup)
      (mma-accumulate-via-tile (16 8 8) C-tile A-tile B-tile (acc)
        (accum-op)
        ;; ILLEGAL: this is the REDUCTION body, so acc is a partial sum.  The correct site is
        ;; the :epilogue below, where the C-tile is complete (that is rung 03).
        (map-elements! acc #'double-val))
      (sync-workgroup)
      :epilogue
      (store-tile C-tile C (grid-y grid-x)))))
